Now I understand the export test structure. Now, the CLI boot path: where should the boot-time TTL sweep go for the CLI host? Let me look at where the `TrajectoryRequestBlobStore` is created in trajectory_commands.dart — `_onModelRequest`. The suggestion says "on host boot (or session open), sweep every sibling requests/ dir under .fah/trajectories/ against the same TTL".

Let me design: add to `TrajectoryRequestBlobStore` a static method or instance method `sweepExpiredAll()` that lists `.fah/trajectories/*/requests` and applies the TTL to each. Actually cleaner: add a method `sweepExpired({required String? sessionId})` — hmm.

Let me design the API:

```dart
/// Sweps expired blobs (age past [ttlDays]) in EVERY session's request
/// dir under `.fah/trajectories/` — the boot-time reaper ...
Future<int> sweepExpiredEverywhere() async {
 final root = '.fah/trajectories';
 final listing = await _env.listDir(root);
 final dirs = listing.valueOrNull ?? const <FileInfo>[];
 var deleted = 0;
 for (final entry in dirs) {
   if (!entry.isDirectory) continue; // check FileInfo shape
   deleted += await enforceBudget(dir: '${root}/${entry.name}/requests');
 }
 return deleted;
}
``

Need to change `enforceBudget` to accept an optional dir parameter: `Future<int> enforceBudget({String? dir})`. But `enforceBudget` currently uses the newest-always-kept semantics: `for (var i = 0; i < sorted.length - 1; i++)` — for an EMPTY dir of a dead session (only one file, expired), the newest-kept rule would keep 1 file forever. Hmm — for TTL sweep of dead sessions, should the last (expired) file also go? The thread says "30-day-old blobs of dead sessions stay forever". If a dead session has exactly one blob aged 40 days, the current enforceBudget keeps it (newest always kept). For a boot-time TTL sweep, expired means expired — the ticket says blobs older than TTL should go. The rotation rule "newest always kept" exists so a live session never rotates away its just-written blob within enforceBudget. For the TTL-only sweep across dead sessions, expired files should all go regardless.

So better: implement the boot sweep as its own pass: `sweepExpired({String? dir})` that deletes files older than TTL, regardless of budget/newest-kept. And make `enforceBudget` internally use the same age predicate. Actually simplest: refactor age filtering into a helper; `enforceBudget` keeps its semantics; add public `sweepExpiredEverywhere()`:

```dart
